, because 3M warns that gelcoat that cracks will crack the film with it.</p>\n<h3>Awnings, ladders and swing-out carriers</h3>\n<p>Side awnings bolt along the roof edge, rear ladders and tire or box carriers bolt through the rear doors. Each one either comes off before wrapping or defines where the film stops. Removing them gives a seamless result and is usually worth the extra time on a vehicle you plan to keep.</p>\n<h3>Repainted panels</h3>\n<p>Used vans often have at least one repainted panel from a previous life. Film over fresh or poorly cured paint can bubble, and Avery Dennison does not warrant its films over aftermarket paint. Tell the shop which panels were resprayed so they can test adhesion rather than discover the problem.</p>\n\n<h2>Colour Change, Two-Tone or Graphics</h2>\n<p>Three approaches cover most camper van wrap projects:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Full colour change.</strong> Every painted panel in one film. On a high-roof van this is a large job with long vertical panels and at least one seam on the sides; a tall van''s sides are wider than a roll of film, so seams are placed low and run with the body lines.</li>\n<li><strong>Two-tone.</strong> A contrasting lower band below the body line, often in a darker or textured film, with the factory white above. It covers the area that takes gravel and mud, costs less than a full wrap, and can be rewrapped on its own.</li>\n<li><strong>Graphics over the factory colour.</strong> Cut vinyl mountain lines, stripes, a name and a logo on the white van, the lightest and cheapest route.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The body-shape rules in our <a href=\"/van-wrap-design-sprinter-transit-promaster/\">van wrap design guide</a> apply here as well: keep detail out of the Sprinter''s body channels, use the Transit''s crease as a divider, and treat the ProMaster''s flat sides as the big canvas they are. For a complete recreational vehicle rather than a van, our <a href=\"/rv-wrap-toronto-class-c-motorhome/\">Class C motorhome wrap</a> shows the same ideas on a larger scale.</p>\n\n<figure><img src=\"/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Good-woof-Sprinter-High-Roof-Full-Wrap-After-Side-1024x538.webp\" alt=\"Mercedes Sprinter high roof van with a full colour wrap\" width=\"1024\" height=\"538\" loading=\"lazy\" /><figcaption>A high-roof Sprinter in a full wrap: long vertical panels, a seam placed low, and the roof left to carry its equipment.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h2>Gravel Roads, Campsites and Keeping the Wrap Alive</h2>\n<p>A camper van wrap goes where work vans do not: forestry roads, beaches, salted winter highways on the way to a ski hill. A few habits keep a camper van wrap looking good:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Protect the lower panels.</strong> Gravel chips film as readily as paint. A darker lower band hides chips; paint protection film on the lower panels and behind the wheels stops them. Our <a href=\"/ppf-vs-vinyl-wrap-difference/\">PPF vs vinyl wrap</a> comparison explains the difference.</li>\n<li><strong>Wash by hand, and soon.</strong> Tree sap, bird droppings and bug splatter etch film if they sit in the sun. Campsite hose water and a soft mitt beat a pressure washer at a truck stop, and pressure washers should stay well back from edges and seams.</li>\n<li><strong>Mind the snow.</strong> Brush, do not scrape; our guide to <a href=\"/remove-ice-and-snow-from-a-wrapped-vehicle/\">removing ice and snow from a wrapped vehicle</a> has the method.</li>\n<li><strong>Check the edges each season.</strong> Around roof fittings, window trim and door edges, where a lifted corner caught early is a two-minute fix.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Our <a href=\"/top-8-maintenance-tips-for-vehicle-wraps/\">maintenance tips</a> cover the full routine.</p>\n\n<h2>Timing the Wrap in a Conversion</h2>\n<p>The best moment for a camper van wrap is after the bodywork and window cutting are finished and cured, and before roof equipment, awnings and carriers are mounted. That gives the installer clean, complete panels and lets the hardware go on over the film. If the van is already built out, the wrap can still be done; it simply involves more removal and more trimming. Either way the van needs to be indoors at shop temperature, which in the GTA means a heated bay for much of the year; see our <a href=\"/best-time-vehicle-wrap-toronto/\">guide to wrap timing in Toronto</a>.</p>\n\n<h2>Give Your Van a Colour of Its Own</h2>\n<p>Vinyl Wrap Toronto wraps Sprinter, Transit and ProMaster conversions in 3M and Avery Dennison cast films, from a two-tone lower band to a full colour change with custom graphics, and plans every roof fitting and window before the film is cut. See our <a href=\"/van-wraps/\">van wraps</a> and <a href=\"/recreational-wrap-toronto-2/\">recreational wraps</a> pages, then <a href=\"/contact/\">request a quote</a> or call <a href=\"tel:416-746-1381\">416-746-1381</a>.</p>\n\n<p><em>Sources: 3M Instruction Bulletin, <a href=\"https://multimedia.3m.com/mws/media/2419614O/3m-instruction-bulletin-application-substrate-selection-and-preparation-ra-pdf.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Application: Substrate Selection and Preparation</a> (fibreglass outgassing test, gelcoat, rubber and caulking); Avery Dennison <a href=\"https://graphics.averydennison.com/content/dam/averydennison/graphics/ap/en/Instructional-Bulletins/ib-1.01-substrate-cleaning-and-preparation-ap-en.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Instructional Bulletin 1.01</a> (aftermarket paint).</em></p>\n","style":"text-align:start;font-family:\"Poppins\", Sans-serif;font-size:18px;line-height:1.5em;font-weight:300"}]},{"width":25,"background":"#F8F8F8","padding":"10px 10px 10px 10px","blocks":[{"type":"toc","title":"Table of Contents","box":"background-color:#F8F8F8"},{"type":"form"}]}]}]}]', datetime('now')
